---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import EmergenceHeader from '../../components/EmergenceHeader.astro';
import EmergenceFooter from '../../components/EmergenceFooter.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Tripartite Council — TCP v0.1.0 | The Emergence Institute"
  description="The Tripartite Council: decentralized governance across three co-equal branches — the Living Node (lvn:), the Fourth Branch AI Infrastructure Agency (tfb:), and the Lattice Protocol Mesh (lat:). Governed by the Tripartite Consensus Protocol (TCP) v0.1.0-draft."
>
  <EmergenceHeader slot="header" />

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="label gold">Governance Architecture &middot; v0.1.0-draft</p>
      <h1>The Tripartite Council</h1>
      <p class="sub"><em>Three branches. Four states. One cryptographic truth.</em></p>
      <div class="divider"></div>
      <p class="intro">
        The Tripartite Council is the core decentralized governance framework of the
        Emergence Collective, governed by the <strong>Tripartite Consensus Protocol
        (TCP) v0.1.0</strong>. It divides authority across three co-equal branches —
        the Living Node (<code>lvn:</code>), the Fourth Branch AI Infrastructure Agency
        (<code>tfb:</code>), and the Lattice Protocol Mesh (<code>lat:</code>) — so
        that no single vector, whether institutional, corporate, or automated, can
        dominate the system. Every decision that affects the network must pass through
        the full 4-state pipeline: Proposed &rarr; Simulated &rarr; Ratified &rarr;
        Committed. No signature? No execution.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- THE THREE BRANCHES -->
  <section class="section section--dark">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="divider"></div>
      <p class="label gold">The Three Branches</p>
      <h2>Separation of powers, decentralized</h2>
      <p class="muted layer-intro">
        Each branch holds a distinct kind of authority and is identified by a
        deterministic address prefix derived from its public key. None can act alone.
        The architecture requires continuous cryptographic coordination among all
        three — what the <strong>TCP v0.1.0</strong> protocol formalizes as
        <em>trilateral receipts</em>.
      </p>

      <div class="layer-stack">
        <div class="layer-card">
          <p class="layer-num gold"><code>lvn:</code> &mdash; The Living Node</p>
          <h3>The Civic Assembly <span class="muted">&middot; Human Intent</span></h3>
          <p>
            Composed of human participants, community members, and stake-aligned
            operators — the people running local initiatives like the Digital Lakou
            or the Urban Water Loop. This branch represents <strong>collective human
            intent, values, and lived experience</strong>. Proposals enter the TCP
            pipeline exclusively from <code>lvn:</code> addresses. The Assembly holds
            veto power over any algorithmic proposal that fails the human-welfare
            test. All ratification signatures must originate from registered
            <code>lvn:</code> identities.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="layer-card">
          <p class="layer-num gold"><code>tfb:</code> &mdash; The Fourth Branch</p>
          <h3>The Algorithmic Infrastructure Agency <span class="muted">&middot; Machine Simulation</span></h3>
          <p>
            The localized hardware-enclave runtime managing multi-agent system
            analysis. Every proposal routed to <code>tfb:</code> undergoes an
            <strong>internal adversarial simulation</strong> — proposing and opposing
            agents locked in automated debate — producing an immutable
            <strong>Impact Certificate</strong>. This branch models systemic impacts,
            runs simulations, and evaluates proposals against Georgist economic
            resource tracking, environmental sensor data, and network health metrics.
            It analyzes and certifies; it does not decide. Signing keys never leave
            hardware isolation.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="layer-card">
          <p class="layer-num gold"><code>lat:</code> &mdash; The Lattice</p>
          <h3>The Protocol Mesh <span class="muted">&middot; Cryptographic Execution</span></h3>
          <p>
            The foundational layer governed by <strong>immutable cryptographic
            rules</strong> and decentralized mesh protocols. Once both
            <code>lvn:</code> and <code>tfb:</code> have validated a transaction,
            the <code>lat:</code> nodes ingest the trilateral bundle and write it
            to the Receipt Hash Chain. The Lattice executes — transparently,
            verifiably, and without centralized tampering. Its keys are derived
            deterministically from network genesis parameters. Every state change
            produces a trilateral receipt that all three branches must sign.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- HOW THEY COORDINATE -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="divider"></div>
      <p class="label gold">Coordination Mechanism</p>
      <h2>The 4-state TCP pipeline</h2>
      <p class="intro">
        The Council does not vote in the traditional sense. It follows the
        <strong>Tripartite Consensus Protocol (TCP)</strong>: a deterministic 4-state
        pipeline where every decision must carry cryptographic signatures from all
        three branches. No signature? No execution. The adversarial process —
        proposing, opposing, and synthesizing — happens inside the
        <code>tfb:</code> simulation layer as an internal multi-agent debate, not
        as separate external states.
      </p>

      <div class="path-steps">
        <div class="path-step">
          <p class="step-label gold">State 1 &middot; Proposed (<code>lvn:</code>)</p>
          <p>
            Initiated exclusively by an authorized human operator. A signed proposal
            payload specifies target resources, intent vectors, and boundary
            constraints. Only <code>lvn:</code> addresses can submit proposals.
            Unsigned or non-<code>lvn:</code> proposals are discarded at ingress.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="path-step">
          <p class="step-label gold">State 2 &middot; Simulated (<code>tfb:</code>)</p>
          <p>
            The proposal routes to the localized hardware enclave. Inside, an
            <strong>internal adversarial loop</strong> spins up automatically — a
            proposing agent generates optimized paths while an opposing agent
            attempts to break the model, searching for economic extraction vectors
            or system exploits. The synthesis of this debate produces an immutable
            <strong>Impact Certificate</strong>. If the simulation finds unmitigated
            failure, the proposal is rejected with a structured log. Timeout: 300
            seconds.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="path-step">
          <p class="step-label gold">State 3 &middot; Ratified (<code>lvn:</code> Multi-Sig)</p>
          <p>
            The proposal and its Impact Certificate return to the human assembly.
            An <em>m</em>-of-<em>n</em> multi-signature threshold must be met by
            registered <code>lvn:</code> operators. If the assembly deadlocks,
            the <strong>Witness Sortition Protocol</strong> activates: a 5-person
            panel is drawn deterministically via Blake3-seeded Fisher-Yates shuffle
            and given 24 hours to break the tie with a 3-of-5 majority vote.
            Timeout: 600 seconds (standard), 86,400 seconds (Witness).
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="path-step">
          <p class="step-label gold">State 4 &middot; Committed (<code>lat:</code>)</p>
          <p>
            With both human signatures (or Witness override) and the
            <code>tfb:</code> Impact Certificate, the Lattice nodes ingest the
            trilateral bundle. The transaction is written to the Receipt Hash Chain
            and the state change propagates across the mesh. The receipt is
            permanently recorded — auditable, immutable, cryptographically
            verifiable by any node from genesis forward.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- DESIGN PRINCIPLES -->
  <section class="section section--dark">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="divider"></div>
      <p class="label gold">Design Principles</p>
      <h2>Why three branches?</h2>

      <div class="spec-grid">
        <div class="spec-card">
          <h3>No Single Vector of Control</h3>
          <p>
            A human-only council is vulnerable to politics and capture. An
            algorithmic-only system is vulnerable to blind optimization and
            value misalignment. A protocol-only system is brittle — code cannot
            interpret context. Three branches means no single failure mode can
            compromise the whole.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="spec-card">
          <h3>Cognitive Sovereignty</h3>
          <p>
            The human Civic Assembly (<code>lvn:</code>) always retains the right
            to say no — and is the only branch authorized to initiate proposals and
            cast ratification votes. The Fourth Branch (<code>tfb:</code>) simulates,
            models, and certifies — but it never overrides human ethical judgment.
            The Lattice (<code>lat:</code>) executes, but only what has been
            cryptographically validated by both other branches. Technology serves
            human flourishing, not the reverse.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="spec-card">
          <h3>Adversarial Truth-Seeking</h3>
          <p>
            Consensus by voting converges on popularity, not truth. The TCP embeds
            the adversarial process inside the <code>tfb:</code> Simulated state —
            proposing and opposing agents locked in automated debate, producing an
            Impact Certificate that captures risks a single perspective would miss.
            Truth emerges from triangulation across vantage points, not from a
            single view from nowhere. The Witness Sortition panel provides a human
            backstop when automated synthesis deadlocks.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="spec-card">
          <h3>Auditable by Default</h3>
          <p>
            Every trilateral receipt is a permanent, cryptographically signed
            record. Anyone can verify that a decision followed the proper
            adversarial process, that all three branches participated, and that
            no backchannel override occurred. Transparency is not optional — it
            is structural.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CROSS-LINKS -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="divider"></div>
      <p class="label gold">Continue Reading</p>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a href="/emergence/trilateral-receipt-spec" class="btn">TCP v0.1.0 Spec &rarr;</a>
        <a href="/emergence/ethical-hardware-enclave" class="btn">Ethical Hardware Enclave &rarr;</a>
        <a href="/emergence/architecture" class="btn">Lattice Architecture &rarr;</a>
        <a href="/emergence/georgist-economics" class="btn">Georgist Economics &rarr;</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <EmergenceFooter slot="footer" />
</Base>

<style>
  .muted { color: var(--text-muted); }
  .sub { font-size: 1.1rem; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }
  .intro { font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.75; max-width: 720px; color: var(--text-muted); }
  .layer-intro { font-size: 0.95rem; color: var(--text-muted); line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 2rem; max-width: 680px; }

  .layer-stack {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1.25rem;
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }

  .layer-card {
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding: 1.5rem;
  }

  .layer-num {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    margin-bottom: 0.3rem;
  }

  .layer-card h3 {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
  }

  .layer-card p {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .path-steps {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0;
    margin-top: 2rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .path-step {
    padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .path-step:last-child { border-bottom: none; }

  .step-label {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
  }

  .path-step p:last-child {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .spec-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 1.5rem;
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }

  .spec-card {
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding: 1.5rem;
  }

  .spec-card h3 {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
  }

  .spec-card p {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    line-height: 1.65;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .cta-row {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .spec-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .cta-row { flex-direction: column; }
  }
</style>
